<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Claude Monet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1840">1840</a>–<a href="/years/1926">1926</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Oscar-Claude Monet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Claude Monet was a French painter and a founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/impressionism">Impressionism</a>, its most consistent practitioner in painting nature as he perceived it, above all in outdoor landscapes. Raised in Le Havre, he was introduced to plein air painting by Eugène Boudin and studied under Charles Gleyre alongside Auguste Renoir. The movement's name comes from his Impression, Sunrise, shown in 1874 at the First Impressionist Exhibition, which he and like-minded artists organised as an alternative to the Salon. From 1883 he lived at Giverny, where he made a water-lily pond, and he painted the same scenes many times to catch the changing light: haystacks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> Cathedral and the water lilies of his last 20 years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/claude-monet.jpg" alt="Claude Monet" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nadar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Claude_Monet_1899_Nadar_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The name came from a joke: in a satirical review of the 1874 exhibition in Le Charivari, the critic Louis Leroy played on the title of <b>Claude Monet</b>’s Impression, Sunrise and wrote that wallpaper in its embryonic state was more finished than that seascape.</blockquote><a href="/movement/impressionism">Impressionism · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
